#edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294195 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294195 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ae73e0e-4aab-5fee-a08f-9c1fc5417de2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294497 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294497 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294497 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294497 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294497 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9d3f8d39-95a5-593d-a567-b7d92d998b80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294549 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294549 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294549 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294549 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294549 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac0ed6ee-35ca-53af-b996-1a23be08d47f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294705 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294705 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294705 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294705 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294705 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dde32572-8787-55e0-b07b-cc51561e3c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{3x-12}\\,?$\n\nThe domain is\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup (${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81159dc4-76c4-50b0-ae3d-b2812d3bd662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294843 #edb/ref 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-12 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294851 #edb/ref 1071 "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{kx+4}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e31c6b4-744c-5f6b-83bb-dc39caa8f302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294851 #edb/ref 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+4 & =0 \\\\ kx & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{4}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{4}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{k} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294853 #edb/ref 1071 "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+7}{bx+5}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{6})\\cup (-\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ),$ then $b=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2acc18f-6649-5da5-99ec-a581a511537c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294853 #edb/ref 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}bx+5 & =0 \\\\ bx & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{5}{b}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=-\\frac{5}{6}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{5}{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{b} & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{5}{b} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186293941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{9}{2x+9}+5\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f1e9d48-4b9a-5415-83bb-4467d8d9a1d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294860 #edb/ref 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=5.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=5,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,5)\\cup (5,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0da9b33dde51888d770d9e095d74b628698d870727a53f252948d99644c7bc3d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364709 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364709 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364709 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364709 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{4-x}-3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e883836-cad7-580b-9e8a-24fa830bfc9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364709 #edb/ref 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-3.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1b6683bab3caf6fc2350c14382266483c848e6b0490d4d1b7cf6c5f5d2ad273.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364710 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9a0489fd-3135-5e0f-bd36-ffa3b3553d6f" #edb/ref 175921860